// Viṣṇu Sahasranāma — concepts 901–1000 (English, own paraphrases).
// Titles are IAST nominatives in Śaṅkara-tradition numbering; summaries
// condense the Śaṅkara–Satyabhāṣyam readings in this project's own words.
// See vishnu-sahasranama-source-provenance.ts.
export const vsConcepts9011000: any[] = [
  { id: "vs-nama-901", title: "Svastidaḥ", category: "Bow-Sky", summary: "The giver of blessing. Svastida opens the fivefold benediction: welfare bestowed, well-being dealt — the Undecaying (previous name) now distributing, eternity turning economist. Giving precedes making, enjoying and lavishing in this verse's grammar: first the gift leaves His hand, then the economy unfolds. Benediction here is not flattery but substance — safety, health, rightness conveyed. Receive one good today as His giving, and pass one on. (Stotra verse 96.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-096"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-902", title: "Svastikṛt", category: "Bow-Sky", summary: "The maker of blessing. Svastikṛt works weal: welfare wrought — giving (previous name) backed by making, the gift guaranteed by manufacture, no blessing outsourced or accidental. The tradition distinguishes the two: dā gives the good, kṛt constructs the conditions for good, providence as both donor and artisan. Made blessing lasts; merely wished blessing fades. Make one condition for another's welfare today — arrange, prepare, provide. (Stotra verse 96.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-096"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-903", title: "Svasti", category: "Bow-Sky", summary: "Blessing itself, auspiciousness embodied. Svasti is benediction being: fortune founded — not giving or making welfare but being it, the noun behind the verbs, welfare's substance before its circulation. The verse's centre holds stillness: giver and maker flank what they give and make, means surrounding meaning. All auspicious words (svasti-vacana) borrow weight from this mint. Rest once in bare blessedness without listing its items. (Stotra verse 96.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-096"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-904", title: "Svastibhuk", category: "Bow-Sky", summary: "The enjoyer of blessing. Svastibhuk tastes welfare: weal relished — the Giver and Maker (previous names) now savouring what They circulate, consummation completing economy. Enjoying proves the blessing pure: He can partake without depleting, taste without snatching, His share increasing the stock. Auspiciousness is not spent by use but proved by it. Enjoy one lawful good fully today, without guilt, as His rite. (Stotra verse 96.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-096"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-905", title: "Svastidakṣiṇaḥ", category: "Bow-Sky", summary: "The lavish distributor of blessing. Svastidakṣiṇa broadcasts bounty: weal widely dealt — giving, making, being, enjoying culminate in lavishness, welfare administered at festival scale, the dakṣiṇā (priestly gift) that overflows measure. The verse closes benediction's economy in generosity: containment would contradict the Endless proclaimed just before. Liberality is infinity's etiquette. Give one blessing lavishly today — beyond proportion, beyond return. (Stotra verse 96.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-096"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-906", title: "Araudraḥ", category: "Wonder", summary: "The never-cruel. Araudra cancels cruelty: harshness halted — the new verse's doorway, gentleness announced before arms (earrings and discus follow), so that every weapon hereafter is read as tenderness equipped. Never-cruel is not softness but vow: power solemnly declining ferocity, omnipotence refusing the cruel option. Terror (elsewhere) serves rescue; cruelty serves nothing and is abjured. Renounce one cruelty — of word, of wit, of silence — today. (Stotra verse 97.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-097"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-907", title: "Kuṇḍalī", category: "Wonder", summary: "The earringed, wearing shining ear-globes. Kuṇḍalin enamels ears: hearing hallowed — beauty stationed at the organ of listening, ornament testifying that He hears before He strikes. The verse pairs earrings with discus: gentleness armed, adornment alongside armament, the Listener who is also the Legion. Makara-kuṇḍalas catch light as a promise that prayer lands. Listen once beautifully today — adorned attention for an unadorned speaker. (Stotra verse 97.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-097"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-908", title: "Cakrī", category: "Wonder", summary: "The discus-bearer. Cakrin wheels time: cycles chauffeured — Sudarśana, the lovely-to-behold, spinning as order's instrument, dharma's wheel that severs (demon throats) and shelters (devotee vows) by turns. Earrings (previous name) listen; the wheel acts: audition ripening into intervention, never-cruelty (verse's opening) wielding precision instead of passion. The wheel returns to its hand — force that never escapes control. Trust one turning of His wheel in your affairs. (Stotra verse 97.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-097"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-909", title: "Vikramī", category: "Wonder", summary: "The firm-striding, most daring (named again). Vikramin re-dares: courage re-cheered — the three strides recalled (Vāmana covering cosmos), daring measured in worlds, not gestures. Repetition places stride among gentlenesses: never-cruel, earringed, yet covering heaven in two steps — tenderness with infinite reach. His daring is devotees' safety: strides that evict Bali enthrone Prahlāda's God. Take one firm stride today where you have been shuffling. (Stotra verse 97.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-097"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-910", title: "Ūrjitaśāsanaḥ", category: "Wonder", summary: "Whose command is mighty. Ūrjitaśāsana weights writs: edicts enforced — stride (previous name) speaking, movement maturing into mandate, the march issuing orders that creation obeys. Might here is not volume but validity: His command effects because His being backs it, energy (ūrjas) as legislation's engine. Orders that stride keep marching: dharma's decrees advance like the strides that uttered them. Obey one command of conscience mightily today. (Stotra verse 97.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-097"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-911", title: "Śabdātigaḥ", category: "Wonder", summary: "Beyond all words. Śabdātiga tops talk: language left — the Commander (previous name) exceeding the medium of command, scripture's Lord surpassing scripture's syllables, as the Kaṭha says the Self is beyond speech yet reached through it. Transcendence here guards might from literalism: no edict exhausts the Edicter, no mandate maps the Mandator. Words point; He exceeds; pointing still works. Honour one silence today as His beyond. (Stotra verse 97.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-097"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-912", title: "Śabdasahaḥ", category: "Wonder", summary: "The endurer of words. Śabdasaha welcomes utterances: declarations deemed — the Beyond-word (previous name) nevertheless bearing words, transcendence that still listens, infinity tolerating invocation. Enduring differs from needing: He suffers names, hymns, even complaints, the way the ocean suffers rivers — without rising. The pair holds mystery and mercy: past speech, yet present to speech. Address Him once in words, trusting He endures them gladly. (Stotra verse 97.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-097"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-913", title: "Śiśiraḥ", category: "Wonder", summary: "The cool, as winter dew. Śiśira comforts chill: cold cherished — command and transcendence (previous names) cooling into balm, authority's noon tempered by mercy-dawn, the fevered world sponged. Coolness here is not indifference but therapy: heat of sin, of grief, of wrath allayed by contact, as dew revives the trampled grass without lecture. Day commanded; dew consoles. Cool one fever — your own or another's — gently today. (Stotra verse 97.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-097"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-914", title: "Śarvarīkaraḥ", category: "Wonder", summary: "The maker of night. Śarvarīkara knits darkness: rest readied — coolness (previous name) extended into nightfall, the Commander dimming lamps so labourers sleep, creation given its sabbath in every rotation. Night is not negation but nurse: dreams mended, weariness washed, the day's accounts closed in mercy. The verse rests what the commands roused — mighty edicts tucked under darkness. Keep one night's rest unrobbed, yours or another's. (Stotra verse 97.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-097"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-915", title: "Akrūraḥ", category: "Wonder", summary: "The never-cruel, supremely gentle. Akrūra continues gentleness as person: cruelty's absence become a devotee's name recalled — Akrūra the Vṛṣṇi messenger, gentleness with a biography, proving the attribute walks. Araudra (two verses past) denied cruelty; akrūra embodies its opposite, denial ripened into character. Skill and patience (following names) are this gentleness deployed. Practise one gentleness with a human face today. (Stotra verse 98.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-098"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-916", title: "Peśalaḥ", category: "Wonder", summary: "The supremely soft and deft. Peśala tempers tenderness: gentleness graduated — softness skilled, delicacy precise, the surgeon's touch that is both tender and exact. Gentleness (previous name) without deftness bruises; deftness without gentleness cuts: this name weds them, tact as theology. The verse pairs soft with clever (next names): tenderness that knows its craft. Handle one fragile thing — mood, confidence, heirloom — deftly today. (Stotra verse 98.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-098"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-917", title: "Dakṣaḥ", category: "Wonder", summary: "The clever and efficient (named again). Dakṣa re-skills: dexterity re-doubled — softness (previous name) proving competent, tenderness passing examination, the deft gentle One also the able One. Repetition in the gentleness verse refuses the cult of amiable incompetence: kindness here can also manage, construct, complete. Efficiency is gentleness honed. Execute one task cleverly today as an act of devotion. (Stotra verse 98.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-098"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-918", title: "Dakṣiṇaḥ", category: "Wonder", summary: "The most liberal and gracious (named again). Dakṣiṇa re-lavishes: bounty re-bountied — skill (previous name) opening into liberality, competence flowering as generosity, the right hand (dakṣiṇa) that gives without the left auditing. Graciousness is skill's surplus: ability exceeding duty becomes gift. The verse's arc runs gentle-deft-gracious: character, craft, largesse. Give one thing beyond the invoice today. (Stotra verse 98.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-098"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-919", title: "Kṣamiṇāṃ varaḥ", category: "Wonder", summary: "The greatest among the forbearing. Kṣamiṇāṃvara crowns patience: forgiveness first — graciousness (previous name) tested by offence and passing, liberality extended to the undeserving, pardon as eminence. Best-of-the-patient means patience has ranks and He tops them: every human pardon a foothill of His range. Forbearance here flowers into fearlessness (next name): the pardoning need not dread. Forgive foremost once today. (Stotra verse 98.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-098"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-920", title: "Vidvattamaḥ", category: "Wonder", summary: "The greatest among the wise. Vidvattama superlatives learning: lore's climax — patience (previous name) proving learned, forgiveness as the highest syllabus, wisdom's crown set on forbearance rather than on dialectic. Wisest-of-wise terminates debate's ladder in character: knowing culminates in pardoning. The learned who cannot forgive have stopped a rung short. Learn one thing today that makes you kinder. (Stotra verse 98.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-098"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-921", title: "Vītabhayaḥ", category: "Wonder", summary: "The fear-free. Vītabhaya departs dread: fright fled — wisdom and pardon (previous names) proving fearless, the crowned virtues needing no guard, perfection without patrol. Fearlessness here is fruit, not feat: the patient and wise are safe because they threaten nothing and comprehend much. Terror ends where gentleness-skill-wisdom converge. Surrender one dread today to this convergence. (Stotra verse 98.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-098"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-922", title: "Puṇyaśravaṇakīrtanaḥ", category: "Wonder", summary: "Whose hearing and praise purify. Puṇyaśravaṇakīrtana sacraments recitation: hymnists heaped — this very chanting made sacrament, audition and utterance both detergent, the singer washed by the song. The verse closes gentleness-wisdom in practice: all prior names become usable — hear them, sing them, be cleansed. Fame here is functional: renown that rinses. Praise once today to be purified, not to be heard. (Stotra verse 98.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-098"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-923", title: "Uttāraṇaḥ", category: "Crossing", summary: "The ferryman across change's ocean. Uttāraṇa begins the final rescue-arc: drowning drawn up — ferrying as office, crossing as character, the Lord stationed mid-flood with boatmanship perfect. Refuge (early hymn) matures into transport: safety (śaraṇa) grown into transit, the shore coming to meet the swimmer. Force (following names) clears the channel; ferrying is the point. Name one water you need ferrying across. (Stotra verse 99.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-099"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-924", title: "Duṣkṛtihā", category: "Crossing", summary: "The destroyer of evil deeds. Duṣkṛtihan wrecks wrongs: misdeeds mortified — ferrying (previous name) clearing cargo, the boat lightened of guilt-weight before midstream, karma's barnacles scraped. Destroying deeds differs from punishing doers: the act is killed so the actor may live, surgery sparing the patient. Confession boards this boat; concealment capsizes. Offload one misdeed honestly today. (Stotra verse 99.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-099"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-925", title: "Puṇyaḥ", category: "Crossing", summary: "The supremely pure (named again). Puṇya re-hallows mid-rescue: holiness re-hallowed — deed-destroying (previous name) revealing stainlessness, the Ferryman's hands clean enough to lift the stained. Repetition stations purity inside the salvage operation: cleansing is not the far shore but the boat itself, rescue already holy. Purity conveys; impurity clings to wreckage. Touch one pure thing today — water, word, intention — as boarding-pass. (Stotra verse 99.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-099"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-926", title: "Duḥsvapnanāśanaḥ", category: "Crossing", summary: "The destroyer of nightmares. Duḥsvapnanāśana nullifies bad dreams: sleep secured — protection descending past waking into dreaming, the ferry covering even unconscious waters, therapy reaching where will cannot patrol. Nightmares are unconfessed fears rehearsing; His destroying is reassurance administered in the dark. Even sleep is within the rescue-arc. Commit one recurring dread to Him before sleep tonight. (Stotra verse 99.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-099"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-927", title: "Vīrahā", category: "Crossing", summary: "The slayer of hostile heroes (named again). Vīrahan bests braves: hostiles halved — force clearing the ferry's channel, opposition's champions met and mastered, rescue escorted by might. Slaying serves ferrying (verse's opening): the sword is the oar's guard, violence strictly convoy-duty. Heroes of evil fall; heroes of faith are ferried. Stop feeding one hostile strength in yourself today. (Stotra verse 99.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-099"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-928", title: "Rakṣaṇaḥ", category: "Crossing", summary: "The protector. Rakṣaṇa covers creation: cosmos cared — slaying (previous name) settling into guarding, convoy becoming custody, the channel cleared now patrolled. Protection here is continuous, not occasional: watching as state, not as intervention. Nightmares (above) and nature alike fall inside the watch. Rest once inside the patrol instead of posting your own sentries. (Stotra verse 99.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-099"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-929", title: "Santaḥ", category: "Crossing", summary: "The Good, expressed through saintly beings. Sat vests virtue: goodness gentlemen'd — protection (previous name) given human faces, the abstract Good exhibiting in saints as light in lamps. The tradition reads santa as both the Good itself and the good men who manifest it: rescue's crew, the ferryman's assistants. Holiness is contagious through company. Keep company once today with a good person — or be such company. (Stotra verse 99.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-099"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-930", title: "Jīvanaḥ", category: "Crossing", summary: "The life-spark in all creatures. Jīvana lends living: aliveness allotted — goodness (previous name) quickened into vitality, saints' character as universal breath, the ferry's passengers all breathing His loaned air. Life here is not property but allowance: existence issued daily, renewable by remembrance. The life-spark neither ages nor tires; only its housings do. Spend one breath consciously as borrowed life today. (Stotra verse 99.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-099"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-931", title: "Paryavasthitaḥ", category: "Crossing", summary: "The established all around. Paryavasthita stays surrounding: abiding all-around — ferrying's arc closing in omnipresence, rescue not a trip but a residence, the far shore turning out to surround the flood. All-around establishment means arrival everywhere: no outside to the refuge, no margin past the ferry. The crossing ends by abolishing distance. Dwell once today as already arrived. (Stotra verse 99.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-099"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-932", title: "Anantarūpaḥ", category: "Crossing", summary: "The endlessly formed. Anantarūpa figures fathomless: shapes shoreless — all-around establishment (previous name) flowering into infinite exhibition, omnipresence displaying as omniformity, every form a facet of arrival. Endlessness takes faces so the faceless may be met: infinity with features. Form here is hospitality, not limitation. Greet one unfamiliar form — of person, of event — as His exhibition. (Stotra verse 100.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-100"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-933", title: "Anantaśrīḥ", category: "Crossing", summary: "The endlessly glorious. Anantaśrī glories gapless: splendours shoreless — forms (previous name) all radiant, infinity's exhibition never dim, every facet lit. Glory here is not decoration but diffusion: grandeur leaking through every endless form, Lakṣmī innumerable. Where forms could overwhelm, glory harmonizes: multiplicity all shining one light. Admire one splendour today as His instalment. (Stotra verse 100.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-100"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-934", title: "Jitamanyuḥ", category: "Crossing", summary: "Who has conquered anger. Jitamanyu fells fury: temper triumphed — endless form and glory (previous names) proving master of temper, infinity with wrath retired, omnipotence declining irritability. Conquered anger differs from absent anger: He has faced provocation (creation supplies it daily) and mastered it, victory, not vacancy. Temper mastered is power civilized. Triumph temperately once today — win without warming. (Stotra verse 100.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-100"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-935", title: "Bhayāpahaḥ", category: "Crossing", summary: "The remover of all fears. Bhayāpaha demolishes dreads: frights finished — anger conquered (previous name) turning to reassurance, the unthreatening Infinite disarming every alarm. Removing fear is anger-mastery's dividend: only the wrathless can be wholly trusted, only the trusted wholly calm. His fearlessness (elsewhere) becomes our fearlessness here. Transfer one terror to the Remover today. (Stotra verse 100.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-100"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-936", title: "Caturaśraḥ", category: "Crossing", summary: "The four-square, dealing squarely. Caturaśra joints justice true: dealings direct — fear removed (previous name) grounding in fairness, reassurance squared into rectitude, the level altar, the honest die, conduct foursquare. Four-squareness is integrity geometric: no leaning side, no hidden bevel, every face true. Depth (next name) will keep squareness from shallowness. Deal once directly today — no angle, no bevel. (Stotra verse 100.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-100"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-937", title: "Gambhīrātmā", category: "Crossing", summary: "The deep-souled, profound in nature. Gambhīrātman deifies depth: deepness divine — squareness (previous name) given fathom, integrity deepened past surface-honesty into oceanic Selfhood. Depth here is not obscurity but capacity: holding pearls and wrecks alike without turbulence, profundity that steadies. The verse's geometry (square, quarters, directions) roots in this abyss of calm. Sound one depth in yourself today instead of skimming. (Stotra verse 100.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-100"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-938", title: "Vidiśaḥ", category: "Crossing", summary: "The ordainer of the quarters. Vidiśas allots alone: portions peerless — depth (previous name) distributing direction, the profound Self assigning every region its quarter, space administered from stillness. Ordaining quarters is orienting the lost: homelessness answered by mapping, wandering by appointment. Every direction has His signature; no one stands nowhere. Face your true quarter today — duty, place, direction — and stand in it. (Stotra verse 100.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-100"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-939", title: "Vyādiśaḥ", category: "Crossing", summary: "The commander of directions. Vyādiśas crowns commands: directives divine — quarters ordained (previous name) now officered, mapping maturing into mission, geography given orders. Commanding differs from ordaining: structure receiving purpose, regions their assignments, every quarter a charge. The compass becomes a commission. Accept one directive — of scripture, of conscience, of duty — as His commissioning. (Stotra verse 100.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-100"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-940", title: "Diśaḥ", category: "Crossing", summary: "Direction itself, advising and enlightening. Dik kindles knowledge: guidance given — ordaining and commanding (previous names) resolving into sheer direction, the Commander become the compass, orders ripened into orientation. He is not only the Giver of directions but the Given: every true course terminating in Him. Geometry of grace closes in geography of God. Ask one direction of Him today and walk it. (Stotra verse 100.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-100"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-941", title: "Anādiḥ", category: "Crossing", summary: "The beginningless (named again). Anādi unoriginates origin: startlessness restated — direction (previous verse's close) grounded in priority, every course preceded by the Courser who was never preceded. Repetition at the new verse's door resets the clock to eternity: priority, support, splendour (this verse's trio) all predating their praise. Beginnings borrow; Beginninglessness lends. Begin one thing today backed by the Beginningless. (Stotra verse 101.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-101"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-942", title: "Bhūrbhuvaḥ", category: "Crossing", summary: "The ground of earth, self-existent support. Bhūrbhuvar grounds groundlessly: standing standingless — beginninglessness (previous name) descending into bearing, priority proving supportive, the First becoming the Floor. Earth names Him by standing on Him: existence's groundfloor, the support needing no support (adhṛta-svadhṛta elsewhere). All weights rest here without receipt. Stand once consciously on His ground. (Stotra verse 101.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-101"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-943", title: "Lakṣmīḥ", category: "Crossing", summary: "Splendour Herself, the abode of wealth. Lakṣmī glories gorgeous: riches resplendent — priority and support (previous names) crowned with radiance, being's groundfloor carpeted in glory, power's housekeeping as beauty. She is His inseparable Śrī, prosperity as relationship rather than possession. Splendour here guards the nursery (terror follows): glory with motherhood inside. Receive one prosperity as relationship today. (Stotra verse 101.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-101"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-944", title: "Suvīraḥ", category: "Crossing", summary: "The greatly heroic. Suvīra vests valour splendidly: heroism handsome — splendour (previous name) proving brave, glory with a sword-arm, beauty that can also defend. Great heroism differs from mere victory (elsewhere): magnitude of spirit, generosity in strength, daring that ennobles witnesses. Terror (following names) is this heroism seen from the wrong side. Act once heroically — for another, at cost — today. (Stotra verse 101.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-101"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-945", title: "Rucirāṅgadaḥ", category: "Crossing", summary: "The bright-braceleted. Rucirāṅgada shines shoulders: arms armoured brightly — heroism (previous name) adorned, valour given bracelets, terror dressed for festival. Ornament here is not vanity but assurance: the strong decorating their strength to signal mastery over it, power so governed it can glitter. Bracelets proclaim the arms need no concealing. Wear one strength ornamentally today — display competence kindly. (Stotra verse 101.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-101"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-946", title: "Jananaḥ", category: "Crossing", summary: "The begetter of all living creatures. Janana brings forth beings: births brokered — adorned heroism (previous names) turning generative, strength becoming fatherhood, the warrior as womb's guardian. Begetting here is delivering: midwifery cosmic, every birth His attendance. Terror guards this nursery (next names): ferocity as maternity's fence. Further one living thing's birth or growth today. (Stotra verse 101.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-101"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-947", title: "Janajanmādiḥ", category: "Crossing", summary: "The first cause of creatures' birth. Janajanmādi broaches beings: origination original — begetting (previous name) traced to source, births begun in the Beginningless (verse's opening), genealogy terminating upward in Him. First-causation is not mechanism but fatherhood at scale: origin with affection inside. Every birthday has His signature as first cause. Trace one blessing to its First Cause today. (Stotra verse 101.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-101"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-948", title: "Bhīmaḥ", category: "Crossing", summary: "The terrible (named again). Bhīma re-terrors: formidability re-formed — begetting (previous names) guarded by dread, the nursery fenced with terror, maternity's kindly fence electrified. Repetition teaches tenderness armed: the Begetter is also the Terror, fatherhood's two faces, rescue's sword beside rescue's cradle. Terror here serves the tender exclusively. Fear one evil — and only evil — wholesomely today. (Stotra verse 101.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-101"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-949", title: "Bhīmaparākramaḥ", category: "Crossing", summary: "Of terrible prowess to enemies. Bhīmaparākrama dreads dreadfully: valour vastly dread — terror (previous name) quantified as prowess, formidability proved in performance, the nursery-guard patrolling. Prowess petrifies only the predatory: lambs sleep, wolves tremble — one energy, two addresses (as with fear made and destroyed elsewhere). Might guards meekness. Stand once, terrible to one tyranny, tender to its victims. (Stotra verse 101.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-101"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-950", title: "Ādhāranilayaḥ", category: "Foundation", summary: "The abode of support. Ādhāranilaya homes holding: bearing basic — terror-prowess (previous verse) settling into shelter, arms becoming architecture, the fortress re-described as residence. Support's abode differs from support itself: not the pillar but the house of pillars, refuge with rooms, protection furnished. Gravity blooms (the verse's theme): weight flowering into dwelling. Abide once in His support instead of inspecting it. (Stotra verse 102.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-102"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-951", title: "Adhātā", category: "Foundation", summary: "Above all authority, owing nothing. Adhātṛ crowns crownless: orders originless — support's abode (previous name) owing its charter to none, supremacy underived, the Householder paying no rent. Above-authority means unaccountable upward, wholly accountable downward: answering to none, answering for all. Rule rootless is responsibility absolute. Exercise one authority as stewardship, not ownership, today. (Stotra verse 102.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-102"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-952", title: "Puṣpahāsaḥ", category: "Foundation", summary: "The flower-smiling. Puṣpahāsa parts petals: buds burst — gravity blooming (verse's theme), support smiling, the Abode breaking into blossom at creation's dawn. Smiling here is cosmogony: the universe as His blooming, stern support flowering into colour and scent. Chuckling blossoms prove power joyful: omnipotence playing petal. Bloom once today — one colour, one scent, one lightness. (Stotra verse 102.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-102"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-953", title: "Prajāgaraḥ", category: "Foundation", summary: "The ever-wakeful. Prajāgara spurns slumber: wakefulness watched — the Blooming (previous name) never nodding over its garden, vigilance perpetual, the Gardener sleepless while flowers sleep. Ever-wakeful differs from merely sleepless: attention as affection, watching as tending, the night-shift of love. His eyes relieve our sentries. Sleep once confidently tonight, posting Him as watchman. (Stotra verse 102.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-102"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-954", title: "Ūrdhvagaḥ", category: "Foundation", summary: "The upward-going. Ūrdhvaga holds height: summit sat — wakefulness (previous name) ascending, vigilance with direction, attention climbing. Upward-going is both His motion and our invitation: the Summit that scales, drawing climbers in its wake, ascent contagious. Heights here are moral before spatial: excellence as altitude. Climb one step — of conduct, of thought — upward today. (Stotra verse 102.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-102"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-955", title: "Satpathācāraḥ", category: "Foundation", summary: "The walker of truth's paths. Satpathācāra routes rightly: way wholesome — ascent (previous name) given a road, climbing channelled into conduct, altitude reached by footpath not by leap. True-path-walking proves height accessible: the Summit keeps trails, excellence has signposts, dharma's map is drawn. Rectitude in stride (the verse's theme): vigilance, ascent, rectitude. Walk one true step upward today. (Stotra verse 102.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-102"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-956", title: "Prāṇadaḥ", category: "Foundation", summary: "The giver of breath and life (named again). Prāṇada re-lends lives: breath re-bestowed — true-path-walking (previous name) breathing travellers onward, conduct oxygenated, the Way providing wind. Repetition stations life-giving on the path itself: journey and sustenance one gift, road with rations. Every inhale is His re-grant. Breathe once on the path as His provision. (Stotra verse 102.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-102"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-957", title: "Praṇavaḥ", category: "Foundation", summary: "The syllable Om (named again). Praṇava re-utters: Om re-sounded — breath given (previous name) voicing itself, life exhaling as sacred syllable, physiology flowering into mantra. The uttered Om addresses Rudra-or-Viṣṇu as sacrifice's lord (as at the hymn's opening): the circle closes, last path-words echoing first ground-words. Sound Om once today as path and goal. (Stotra verse 102.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-102"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-958", title: "Paṇaḥ", category: "Foundation", summary: "The trader, supreme manager of exchange. Paṇa brokers bargains: deals divinely done — Om (previous name) startling into commerce, the metaphysical turning mercantile: He bargains for souls, paying grace, counting devotion as currency. Trading here is mercy pricing itself low: priceless goods for a leaf, a flower, a tear. Strike one bargain with Him today — small offering, immense return. (Stotra verse 102.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-102"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-959", title: "Pramāṇam", category: "Foundation", summary: "The measure and the proof. Pramāṇa warrants walking: evidence ensouled — both logic's proof and life's proportion, the warrant by which knowing knows and the scale by which living measures. The great verse peaks here: epistemology opening the quartet (proof, breath, Self, freedom), philosophy folded inside devotion. He is argument's validity and conduct's yardstick alike. Measure one claim and one conduct by Him today. (Stotra verse 103.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-103"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-960", title: "Prāṇanilayaḥ", category: "Foundation", summary: "The home of breaths. Prāṇanilaya boards breathing: lives lodged — proof (previous name) housing physiology, logic giving lodging to lungs, the Warrant become Hostel. Home differs from giver (elsewhere): donation versus domicile, breath both issued and sheltered. All breaths roost here nightly; no respiration is homeless. Rest one breath homeward today. (Stotra verse 103.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-103"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-961", title: "Prāṇabhṛt", category: "Foundation", summary: "The bearer of breaths. Prāṇabhṛt leads living: vitality vetted — home (previous name) proving custodial, lodging with governance, the Hostel with a Warden. Bearing is ruling extended: breaths carried the way policies are carried — supervised, supplied, sustained. Autonomic life is administered life. Let one involuntary blessing — heartbeat, breath — remind you of administration today. (Stotra verse 103.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-103"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-962", title: "Prāṇajīvanaḥ", category: "Foundation", summary: "Who lives by breath, and by whom breath lives. Prāṇajīvana leases life: living lent — bearer (previous name) turning livelihood, custody into catering, the Warden become Provider. The compound runs both ways: He lives by breath (needlessness wearing need) and breath lives by Him (dependence founded). Livelihood is mutual by courtesy, sovereign by truth. Draw one livelihood-breath gratefully today. (Stotra verse 103.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-103"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-963", title: "Tattvam", category: "Foundation", summary: "Reality itself, the Real. Tattva insists is-ly: that-ness thorough — breath-names (previous three) resolving into ontology, physiology flowering as metaphysics, respiration revealing Reality. That-ness is this-ness deepened: everything's is-ness confessed as one Is-ness, the sat of sat-cit-ānanda named at philosophy's peak. Knowing this is freedom's doorway (names complete the arc). Touch one real thing today as Tattva's edge. (Stotra verse 103.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-103"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-964", title: "Tattvavit", category: "Foundation", summary: "The knower of Reality. Tattvavit reports that-ness: reality taught — the Real (previous name) knowing Itself, ontology with self-awareness, Truth conscious of being true. Knowing here is not acquisition but identity: His knowledge is His being, witness without distance. Veda-knower (early hymn) matures here into Reality-knower: scripture's subject become philosophy's self-evidence. Know one truth by being it, not by holding it. (Stotra verse 103.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-103"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-965", title: "Ekātmā", category: "Foundation", summary: "The one and only Self. Ekātman merges me-ness: I-ness indivisible — knower and known (previous names) reconciled in singleness, subject-object's long quarrel settled, the Gītā's thirteenth chapter in one compound. One-Self is not solitary but sole: all selves temporal, this Self eternal; all I's borrowed, this I original. Likeness to Him is unselfing. Release one me-ness — a grievance, a glory — into the One today. (Stotra verse 103.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-103"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-966", title: "Janmamṛtyujarātigaḥ", category: "Foundation", summary: "Beyond birth, death and age. Janmamṛtyujarātiga passes passages: comings, goings, greyings gone beyond — philosophy's peak closing in freedom, proof-breath-Self culminating as liberation, the hymn's epistemology graduating. Beyond is not elsewhere but otherwise: birth, death, age continuing as appearances over the Unaffected. The freed live through passages without being passaged. Live one aging moment as the Beyond's tourist today. (Stotra verse 103.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-103"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-967", title: "Bhūrbhuvaḥsvastaruḥ", category: "Sacrifice", summary: "The tree over earth, sky and heaven. Bhūrbhuvaḥsvastaru trunks tremendous: worlds wooded — one trunk spanning three realms, cosmology as botany, roots in being, branches in becoming, shade over all. The sacrifice-arc opens under foliage: ritual entire sheltered, worship bowered, the cosmic tree hosting the cosmic rite. Botany preaches unity: one trunk, many nests. Shelter once under the spanning tree. (Stotra verse 104.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-104"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-968", title: "Tāraḥ", category: "Sacrifice", summary: "The deliverer (named again). Tāra re-fords floods: crossing re-crossed — the tree (previous name) proving ferry, shade becoming transport, shelter with sailings. Repetition moves delivering into the sacrifice-verse: rescue as ritual's first fruit, salvation scheduled among offerings. Deliverance is not extra-ritual but ritual's point. Accept one ferrying today. (Stotra verse 104.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-104"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-969", title: "Savitā", category: "Sacrifice", summary: "The impeller, father of worlds (named again). Savitṛ re-fathers: worlds re-fathered — deliverance (previous name) given paternity, rescue with lineage, the Ferryman also the Father. Impelling here is begetting extended: arousal ripened into ancestry, Gāyatrī's stimulation grown genealogical. Fathers impel; impellers father — both launch. Launch one good beginning paternally today. (Stotra verse 104.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-104"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-970", title: "Prapitāmahaḥ", category: "Sacrifice", summary: "The great-grandsire of all. Prapitāmaha sires sires: fathers fathered — impelling (previous name) grandparented, paternity given pedigree, Brahmā Himself (the pitāmaha) shown to have a Father's Father. Great-grandsire recedes into affection: authority softened by generation-gap, awe grandparented into indulgence. Lineage terminates upward in tenderness. Honour one grandsire — of blood or of wisdom — today. (Stotra verse 104.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-104"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-971", title: "Yajñaḥ", category: "Sacrifice", summary: "Sacrifice personified (named again). Yajña re-rites: worship re-worked — grandsire (previous name) presiding over oblation, lineage's elder at the altar, tradition's head offering first. Repetition opens sacrifice's quintuple: act, lord, agent, limb, bearer — ritual entire under the tree (verse's opening). He is the rite, not merely its recipient. Work one worship today. (Stotra verse 104.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-104"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-972", title: "Yajñapatiḥ", category: "Sacrifice", summary: "The lord of sacrifice. Yajñapati crowns ceremonies: sessions supervised — sacrifice (previous name) given sovereignty, rite with a ruler, worship administered. Lordship here guarantees validity: offerings under His presidency cannot miscarry, rites He chairs conclude. Presidency is providence applied to altars. Place one undertaking under His presidency today. (Stotra verse 104.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-104"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-973", title: "Yajvā", category: "Sacrifice", summary: "The sacrificer. Yajvan conducts ceremonies: worship worked — lordship (previous name) descending to performance, the President taking the ladle, sovereignty serving. The Lord sacrificing startles: He who receives all offerings also makes them, priest as well as patron, precedent for every offerer. Example authorizes: His sacrificing licenses ours. Offer one thing with His precedent in mind. (Stotra verse 104.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-104"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-974", title: "Yajñāṅgaḥ", category: "Sacrifice", summary: "The limb of sacrifice. Yajñāṅga ministers members: parts participating — sacrificer (previous name) distributing Himself through implements, vessels, verses, fires: every limb of rite a limb of His. Implements are incarnations in miniature: ladle, altar, chant each carrying divinity. Worship's furniture is holy. Handle one tool of your work as His limb today. (Stotra verse 104.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-104"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-975", title: "Yajñavāhanaḥ", category: "Sacrifice", summary: "The bearer of sacrifice. Yajñavāhana works worship out: ceremonies completed — limbs (previous name) given locomotion, rite carried to fruition, sacrifice with a vehicle reaching its destination. Bearing here is completing: what is carried arrives, no offering stranded mid-route. The quintuple closes in conveyance. Carry one good work through to completion today. (Stotra verse 104.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-104"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-976", title: "Yajñabhṛt", category: "Sacrifice", summary: "The upholder of sacrifice. Yajñabhṛt receives rites: worship welcomed — second nonuple opening in support, sacrifice shouldered before it is enjoyed, oblation underpropped. Upholding differs from bearing (previous verse's close): sustaining versus conveying, maintenance versus transport. Altars stand because He stands under them. Uphold one practice — yours or another's — today. (Stotra verse 105.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-105"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-977", title: "Yajñakṛt", category: "Sacrifice", summary: "The maker of sacrifice. Yajñakṛt originates worship: ceremonies commenced — upholding (previous name) given authorship, support traced to invention, rites raised from nothing by His ordinance. Making precedes enjoying (following names): authorship authorizes appetite, the Cook entitled to taste. Every liturgy has His copyright. Originate one offering — of work, of thanks — today. (Stotra verse 105.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-105"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-978", title: "Yajñī", category: "Sacrifice", summary: "The enjoined in sacrifice, its enjoyer. Yajñin savours ceremony: worship tasted — making (previous name) ripening into relish, authorship tasting its work, the Ordainer dining on order. Enjoying proves approving: His pleasure is the rite's validity-stamp, relish as ratification. Worship pleases before it profits. Let one act of yours give Him pleasure before it gives you profit. (Stotra verse 105.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-105"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-979", title: "Yajñabhuk", category: "Sacrifice", summary: "The enjoyer of sacrifice, receiver of all offered. Yajñabhuk gathers givings: presents pocketed — enjoying (previous name) specified as partaking, relish as reception, the Diner collecting the dishes. Receiving completes giving: oblation's circuit closes in His acceptance, worship's arrow landing. No offering sincerely made is declined. Offer one small thing sincerely today. (Stotra verse 105.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-105"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-980", title: "Yajñasādhanaḥ", category: "Sacrifice", summary: "The perfecter of sacrifice. Yajñasādhana works worship out: ceremonies crowned — receiving (previous name) proving fruitful, acceptance maturing into accomplishment, rites realized. Perfecting is more than completing (elsewhere): flaws mended, gaps filled, the crooked rite straightened by His handling. Imperfect offerings arrive perfect through Him. Entrust one flawed effort to His perfecting today. (Stotra verse 105.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-105"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-981", title: "Yajñāntakṛt", category: "Sacrifice", summary: "The fruiter of sacrifice, maker of its end. Yajñāntakṛt finishes finales: ends enacted — perfecting (previous name) given closure, worship worked to fruit, no rite ending void. Fruiting guarantees meaning: rites are investments with certain dividend, labour with assured harvest. The fruiter is faith's banker. Complete one rite — of work, of prayer — expecting fruit. (Stotra verse 105.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-105"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-982", title: "Yajñaguhyam", category: "Sacrifice", summary: "Sacrifice's secret. Yajñaguhya whispers worship: rite's riddle — fruition (previous name) pointing past itself to mystery, ritual's innermost secret kept even from ritualists: that all is food and eater (following names). Secrecy here is depth, not concealment: the obvious rite hiding the stupendous truth in plain sight. Ponder one mystery inside one routine today. (Stotra verse 105.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-105"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-983", title: "Annam", category: "Sacrifice", summary: "Food itself. Anna answers hunger: fare fundamental — secret (previous name) disclosed as dinner, ritual's mystery served on a plate, the cosmos eating itself gratefully. The Taittirīya's food-doctrine (all beings born of food, living by food) stands condensed in one noun: metaphysics edible. Eating is sacrifice's secret because receiving is worship's root. Eat one meal as sacrifice's secret today. (Stotra verse 105.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-105"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-984", title: "Annādaḥ", category: "Sacrifice", summary: "The eater of food. Annāda honours hunger: fare finished — food (previous name) given its consumer, dinner with its diner, the cosmos not only spread but savoured. Eating completes giving: the offered perfected in partaking, worship's circuit closed in digestion. He eats through every eater; every meal is His leftover grace. Eat once gratefully as His guest, and share. (Stotra verse 105.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-105"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-985", title: "Ātmayoniḥ", category: "Sacrifice", summary: "The self-wombed, origin without other. Ātmayoni sources sourceless: womb wombless — dinner's metaphysics (previous names) yielding to origin's mystery, food's cycle grounded in the Uncaused. Self-wombed means self-sufficient: cause and effect coinciding, origin wearing no indebtedness. All wombs borrow; this Womb lends. Rest once in causelessness beneath your causes. (Stotra verse 106.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-106"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-986", title: "Svayañjātaḥ", category: "Sacrifice", summary: "The self-born. Svayañjāta authors arrival: birth borrowed from none — self-wombed (previous name) delivered as self-delivered, origin appearing as advent, eternity consenting to birthday. Self-born differs from unborn (elsewhere): not denying birth but owning it absolutely, begetting oneself. Advents (Kṛṣṇa in the prison-cell, next names) are this name dated. Arrange one new beginning nobly today. (Stotra verse 106.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-106"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-987", title: "Vaikhānaḥ", category: "Sacrifice", summary: "The boar-digger. Vaikhāna mines mud: ground gotten up — self-born (previous name) digging, origin doing excavation, the Varāha tusking the drowned earth from flood. Labour with music (the verse's theme): digging while Sāman sounds (next name), rescue with soundtrack, toil tuneful. The cosmos was salvaged by snout, not by edict. Dig once — into work, into trouble — for another's rescue. (Stotra verse 106.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-106"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-988", title: "Sāmagāyanaḥ", category: "Sacrifice", summary: "The Sāman-singer. Sāmagāyana makes music worshipful: melody ministered — digging (previous name) sung over, labour with anthem, the rescue scored in Sāman cadences. Singing here is not decoration but operation: Sāman effects what it celebrates, melody as instrument. The Boar's snout kept time; creation rose to music. Sing over one labour today. (Stotra verse 106.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-106"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-989", title: "Devakīnandanaḥ", category: "Sacrifice", summary: "The joy of Devakī. Devakīnandana jail-births joy: prison's product — cosmos localized in a cell, infinity in fetters' quarters, the Self-born (verse's opening) dated to midnight, chains, a mother's arms. Cosmic scale collapses into cradle: origin-without-other choosing umbilical cord. Tenderness armed (this zone's refrain) reaches its peak: omnipotence as infant. Delight once helplessly, as Devakī did. (Stotra verse 106.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-106"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-990", title: "Sraṣṭā", category: "Sacrifice", summary: "The creator (named again). Sraṣṭṛ re-crafts: making re-made — prison-joy (previous name) proving cosmogonic, the Infant as Author, cradle as drafting-table. Repetition places creating after the prison-birth deliberately: creation's credentials shown in a cell, omnipotence certified in helplessness. Making costs Him nothing (as at the hymn's opening) yet means everything. Make one small thing freely today. (Stotra verse 106.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-106"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-991", title: "Kṣitīśaḥ", category: "Sacrifice", summary: "The lord of earth. Kṣitīśa lords land kindly: soil supervised — creator (previous name) accepting tenure, authorship taking administration, the rescued earth (Boar, above) now governed. Lordship here is husbandry: ground ruled for fruitfulness, territory as trust. Prison-born, creation-authored, earth-lording: descent completing in dominion. Tend one ground — plot, household, charge — lordlily today. (Stotra verse 106.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-106"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-992", title: "Pāpanāśanaḥ", category: "Sacrifice", summary: "The destroyer of sins. Pāpanāśana shutters sin: offences obliterated — earth-lordship (previous name) cleansing its realm, dominion as detergent, the lullaby's effect (prison-joy's aftermath): guilt gone, cradle-blessing extended to every cradle. Destroying sin differs from damning sinners: stains removed, stained restored. Close one account of guilt today — confess, amend, release. (Stotra verse 106.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-106"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-993", title: "Śaṅkhabhṛt", category: "Sacrifice", summary: "The conch-bearer. Śaṅkhabhṛt sounds summons: call clarified — the armoury-finale opening in proclamation, Pāñcajanya's blast (Kurukṣetra's dawn) announcing that force serves dharma. Conch precedes sword, wheel, bow, mace: declaration before destruction, promise before punishment. His war is always announced; ambush is not His idiom. Sound one clear summons — of truth, of duty — today. (Stotra verse 107.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-107"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-994", title: "Nandakī", category: "Sacrifice", summary: "The sword-bearer, wielding Nandaka. Nandakin draws discrimination: delusion divided — proclamation (previous name) given edge, summons sharpened, joy (nanda) weaponized as insight. The sword severs knots, not throats primarily: doubt, attachment and confusion cut — surgery before warfare. Discrimination is bliss militant. Divide one delusion cleanly today. (Stotra verse 107.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-107"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-995", title: "Cakrī", category: "Sacrifice", summary: "The discus-bearer (named again). Cakrin re-turns time: discus re-discussed — Sudarśana spinning again at the finale, lovely-to-behold closing what earrings opened (verse 97), the wheel that has turned through the whole hymn arriving at the gate. Repetition is inclusio: the armed gentleness framed in its own circle, force that returns to its hand. Trust one turning of time to His hand today. (Stotra verse 107.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-107"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-996", title: "Śārṅgadhanvā", category: "Sacrifice", summary: "The Śārṅga-bowed. Śārṅgadhanvan aims intention: resolve readied — bow bent, string trembling with purpose, Rāma's resolve (verse 92) returning at the close, force educated into aim. The bow lengthens reach: protection at distance, care with range. Bent bow is kept promise: tension held, not released rashly. Aim one intention and hold the draw steady today. (Stotra verse 107.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-107"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-997", title: "Gadādharaḥ", category: "Sacrifice", summary: "The mace-bearer, wielding Kaumodakī. Gadādhara polices power kindly: authority armed — the blunt instrument of grace, crushing (demon skulls) and comforting (devotee heads) by turns, majesty's staff. Mace-first (verse 81) returns last: grace leading with weight, authority that can afford bluntness because aim (previous name) is precise. Carry one authority heavily yet kindly today. (Stotra verse 107.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-107"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-998", title: "Rathāṅgapāṇiḥ", category: "Sacrifice", summary: "The chariot-wheeled in hand. Rathāṅgapāṇi performs promise: weaponlessness weaponed — Kṛṣṇa at Kurukṣetra lifting a wheel against Bhīṣma, vow (not to fight) bent by love, Bhīṣma's delight at being worth the breach. The wheel in hand is devotion's override: love breaking protocol to protect. Protocol serves love, never vice versa. Break one small protocol for love's sake today. (Stotra verse 107.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-107"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-999", title: "Akṣobhyaḥ", category: "Sacrifice", summary: "The unagitated (named again). Akṣobhya re-calms amid arms: poise re-possessed — the armoury (previous six names) carried in perfect calm, force without fever, the thousand names' second unagitatedness (verse 86) returning as seal. Calm carrying force is the hymn's final paradox: omnipotence at rest, arsenal in stillness. Warfare without agitation proves divinity. Carry one weapon — of work, of word — calmly today. (Stotra verse 107.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-107"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-1000", title: "Sarvapraharaṇāyudhaḥ", category: "Sacrifice", summary: "Weaponed with every weapon. Sarvapraharaṇāyudha arms all good: instruments infinite — conch, sword, discus, bow, mace, wheel gathered into universality, no plight lacking its instrument, no assault unanswered. The thousand names close in universal readiness: totality (Viśvam, name 1) maturing into total armament, All-as-universe ending as All-as-arsenal. Face one remaining fear: its weapon is already in His hand. (Stotra verse 107.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-107"] },
]